      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "If $\\frac{4kb}{3}=\\frac{3}{4},$ then $b=$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to isolate $b.$ To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4kb}{3} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot 4kb & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\ 16kb & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $16k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16kb & =9 \\\\ \\frac{16kb}{16k} & =\\frac{9}{16k} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{16k}b}{\\cancel{16k}} & =\\frac{9}{16k} \\\\ b & =\\frac{9}{16k}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
